rows=[(358, 'the-tide-returns-quietly', 'The tide returns quietly', 'Hope follows the tide’s retreat and return. A small advancing edge of water gives that persistence room without a dramatic storm, lighthouse or triumphant sunrise.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 very low-density watercolor shoreline study. Low oblique view of a quiet shallow incoming ripple crossing smooth ordinary sand, a thin broken line of foam begins at lower left and thins toward center right. One earlier wet boundary remains farther up the sand, less distinct and irregular, suggesting repeated return. Pale grey-blue water occupies only part of the frame, warm damp sand and empty soft wash elsewhere; no full sea panorama, horizon, shells, boats, birds, lighthouse, sun or text. Foam is discontinuous and responds to a slight unevenness in the sand, never a lace border; reflections diffuse under overcast daylight. The event is small but persistent, emotionally hopeful without a climax.'), (362, 'a-sky-in-fragments', 'A sky in fragments', 'The displaced speaker tries to build a whole sky from the restricted view of an attic. An oblique roof-window glimpse preserves the physical constraint and the longing for openness without inventing a refugee or city.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 matte charcoal and pale cerulean gouache drawing from inside a modest attic, looking upward. A sloping dark roof plane cuts diagonally across the upper and left edges, with a small off-center rectangular roof opening showing only a portion of ordinary pale blue sky and one incomplete soft cloud. One structural wooden rafter partly interrupts the view, attached consistently to the roof slope. No visible pane reflections needed. Large quiet interior plane to right, softly lit by ambient sky with no projected sun patch. Not a picturesque abandoned room; no decorative cracked plaster, bed, suitcase, maps, person, bird, word or cut-paper collage. The small fragmented sky remains real sky seen through architecture; longing for a larger remembered world.'), (376, 'shade-given-in-the-heat', 'Shade given in the heat', 'The speaker recognizes generous feminine care in the gulmohar seen from a balcony. A canopy offering shade preserves that companionship without drawing an idealized woman or repeating a close flower cluster.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 broad loose gouache canopy study viewed slightly downward from an ordinary balcony height; omit the balcony itself. An off-center gulmohar crown spreads laterally from a partly obscured branching trunk at left. Fine feathery compound foliage appears as overlapping light and dark green masses with scattered small orange-red flower accents, uneven and not a solid floral carpet. Beneath the canopy at lower left is a patch of cool shade on plain dry ground; open pale warm daylight at right. No bird portrait, woman, house, swing, decorative flowers in foreground, writing or exact location claim. Branches carry the canopy plausibly and disappear behind foliage, detail selective. The tree gives rest in summer heat; welcoming rather than spectacular, no cinematic sunbeams.')]
items=[]
